# Line endings: the repo stores LF and every checkout gets LF, on every OS.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true (the windows-latest CI runner included),
# which rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. The fixture tests happen to tolerate
# that, but `prettier --check` then flags every TypeScript file under src/ (its endOfLine
# default is "lf") and the tracked shell scripts stop running under bash. With LF
# everywhere, a Windows-only test failure is a real platform difference, never a
# line-ending artifact.
#
# Every tracked blob is already LF, so adding this file renormalizes nothing.
# When several patterns match a path the last one wins, so the specific rules go below
# the catch-all.

# Default: Git sniffs each file, stores text as LF, and checks it out as LF — eol=lf
# overrides core.autocrlf and core.eol.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Transcript fixtures are split on "\n" and parsed by byte offset; force text + LF instead
# of trusting content sniffing.
*.jsonl text eol=lf

# Vitest snapshots are golden masters; keep their bytes identical on every OS.
*.snap text eol=lf

# SVGs are XML text; LF keeps their bytes identical on every OS.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Images are binary: no line-ending conversion, no text diff or merge
# (`binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff -merge`).
*.png binary
